A||N||15||Zorba||A Racing Story...||||6||
Z||000000||Zorba||08-28-2000||12:35 PM||||So I hear this story yesterday. My friend, who is a SLC Deputy was riding <BR>in his 87 GT down a semi-deserted highway, Sunday afternoon. <BR>He has a stock car. Well, These 3 young guys in a brand new convertible <BR>camaro, make a u-turn behind him and ride next to his passenger side..."want <BR>to race?" - He ignores them by not acknowledging their challenge...remember <BR>he is a traffic cop! <P><BR>They keep up with him at 50MPH and won't give up... After riding next to him <BR>for about 1/2 mile or so, he rolls down his window. They say... <BR>"hey do you want to race or not?" To which he replies: <P>"I am a cop!" - the kid replies: "You're a cop?" You can still race!" <P>HE says: "Don't do something stupid!"   <P>But quickly the excitement on the kid's face must have faded away... <P>He says that he got their license plate number...they rode along on a <BR>separate lane and took the very first exit out... <P>Talking about feeling stupid! <BR>I am pretty sure that one of those guys just told the others: "Watch me smoke that fiero!"<P>You never know who is riding in that fiero or <BR>other car... It's like racing an unmarked patrol car just to have those red <BR>and blue lights right behind you after you WIN!... <P>Doh.... That's why I like the race track. That's where you can show your stuff or shut up.<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Zorba (edited 08-30-2000).]||||reg||6
Z||000001||GT Bastard||08-28-2000||04:27 PM||||That was a painfully boring race story.  Who cares if he's a cop? Hell, it's a deserted highway right?  If anyone gets hurt or killed, it will be their own fault.  Even the passengers know what they are risking when they get into the car with driver.   And taking down their plate #?  How cheesy is that?  They're kids.... If course they're going to screw around in a new sports car. <P>[This message has been edited by GT Bastard (edited 08-28-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by GT Bastard (edited 08-28-2000).]||||reg||13
Z||000002||80's BOY||08-30-2000||12:54 AM||||you make me laugh you, you bastard!!gt bastard that is. i was in a bad mood, now i'm lmfao!! as for the racing story, that thread should've been posted as a "non- racing story". no offense. ||||reg||10
Z||000003||Mattsfiero||08-30-2000||08:07 AM||||Well Gt bastard I find your remarks very rude.... I have lost several friends to bad races ending in an accident...If you want to race then go ahead if you know how to handle your car well anough but if it is against a bunch of kids then someone is either going to get hurt or Killed.. The officer had the right Idea no matter what you may think..Those kids are on there own path to distruction and wether it is a deserted Highway or not he is a Cop and it is his duty in or out of his uiform to protect people.<P>Matt||||reg||3
Z||000004||moonie||08-30-2000||08:50 AM||||hey Zorba , <BR>does Andy let your friend keep his bullet in his gun or in his shirt pocket ?<P>" Aunt Bee , thats a fine fine fiero ! "<P><BR>moonster <P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Zorba||08-30-2000||11:03 AM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by Mattsfiero:<BR>[B]Well Gt bastard I find your remarks very rude....  <P>I did not want to dignify his remarks with an answer but here it goes. First of all, the funny part of the story, to me at least, is that "one rarely expects to challenge a cop for a race,i.e., it was his day off so he wasn't in uniform." Second, if the cop raced and the kids or himself crashed, how stupid would that be? Not to mention a lawsuit and loss of his job? Third, those kids were 18-21 year olds who probably go out challenging other drivers for a race. The way they approached the car could get them killed if they messed with the wrong person. Many people have been shot when provoking a stranger or challenging someone for a duel. The cop acted properly and  taught them a lesson. He did  not report them. He got their license plate# in the event they drove off wrecklessly so he could catch up with them later, in uniform.<BR>Whether you are in a semi-deserted highway or not, it's best to save the race for the track. There you have a chance to see if you are really hot or not. <BR>Most people who think their cars are super fast, end up disappointed at the 1/4 mile runs. My PERSONAL OPINION is simply this: unless your car is a 9-10 second 1/4 miler runner, you should not be bragging about anything. Let alone challenge stranger for road races.<P>There is no doubt that the GT would have "lost" the race with a brand new supercharged camaro and those "kids" would simply be bragging about their victory and they would get a big ego and think they are fast and go challenge someone else.<P>Eventually they'd be in a wheel chair or someone else. Or dead. Or someone else would be dead. I decided long ago to leave the street races for the track. You'd be surprised how many egos are destroyed there in about 8.37 seconds/1/4 mile!<P>Back to the cop. He did the right thing. Did you expect him to race the camaro, break the law he's sworn to uphold and pose a danger to himself, the young guys or others? Can you imagine the headlines? Do you even care? Obviously not. ||||reg||13
Z||000006||HugGRRRFiero||08-30-2000||11:27 AM||||Racing is best off the street.  It's Illegal for one thing and you put yourself and everyone around you at risk.<P>Here's a story.  One time I was driving my '85 "Race" Regal, V6 3.8, absolutly nothing special about the car.  A high school guy and another guy in the passenger seat in one of those ugly two seater capri convertables pulls up next to me at a light.  He started rev'in whatever he had.  I figured what the heck, I'll race him till I reached the speed limit just to show I could out accelerate him.  So we're sitting there, the left hand turn lane arrow turns green, and the guy in the capri pops his clutch and squeals his tires thru the intersection and speeds off as the light is still red!!!  I laughed my a$$ off while I sat and waited for the light to turn green.  What an eeediot, and he was lucky nobody was going thru the intersection at the same time.  Anyway, hope this story wasn't as boring.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">    <BR>    <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A> <p>[This message has been edited by HugGRRRFiero (edited 08-31-2000).]||||reg||4
Z||000007||BN Boomer||08-30-2000||11:33 AM||||Oh brother, like everyone who has ever street raced has wound up either in a wheel chair or dead. Yeah, right. Sorry, but that was a pretty lame story. Next you'll be telling me how bad I am for speeding on I-84 between Trementon and the Idaho border. Now <B>there's</B> a semi-deserted highway!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||80's BOY||08-31-2000||02:48 PM||||zorba, it is admirable that you are defending the officer for his wise decision. it is also admirable that you keep racing off the street. it's dorky kids like that who have ruined the street racing scene in my hometown. racing their mom's minivan and daddy's mustang while causing commotion downtown and such. now this is MY OPINION. you posted this thread as " a racing story". now had you titled it "keep it off the street" or maybe "an unpleasant surprise" or perhaps even " undercover fiero cop" then you probably wouldn't have been flamed with smartass remarks from other people about such a cheesy racing story. when i spend my time to read a thread about racing, i want to hear just that. win or lose, it's about that green light gusto that only occurs when challenged by another car at a stoplight. it makes me shake with adrenaline and shoves my foot through the floorboard to the point that my leg cramps up. me and my car will do the best we can, no matter what i'm up against at the line. so, in MY OPINION if someone posts a thread about racing, it should be about two cars against one another. if you want to post a thread about cops, and there are quite a few officers on this forum, then let us all know by titling the thread appropriately. i'm not trying to tell people how to post threads, but defending those who were really dissapointed by that cheesy, non-racing story posted as a racing story.||||reg||4
Z||000009||Zorba||08-31-2000||03:46 PM||||I appreciate the nice comments. I don't know peoples definitions of a racing story. In my opinion, this story was about racing, thus a racing story. It is the story of 3 idiots wanting to race and running into a plain clothes officer riding his fiero. It's not about an undercover cop in a fiero. It's about a guy riding his fiero who happens to be a cop. Maybe you are referring to threads such as Fiero GT x Camaro type racing...but this was the story about someone who wanted to race and someone who didn't, couldn't and wouldn't. I guess I leave the definition of racing up to the readers but this was about racing, it involved two vehicles, a highway, a powerful (unfair) camaro and a GT. If you want racing stories I can tell you about a guy I know who runs 9.7 seconds in his cobra kitcar and another guy here who runs 8.7 seconds in his blown nova, all of which are faster than our fieros. I also know of v8 fieros beating new corvettes and turbo charged fieros beating honda civics all day long.<BR>But in regards to my title, I guess I could have titled it differently, maybe "3 idiots",<BR>"Obnoxious morons" or some odd thing like that. So I agree with you I could have a different title due to what you and others would expect to read when they see the word<BR>"racing"...||||reg||6
Z||000010||DjKid||08-31-2000||06:21 PM||||My personal opinion, and I'll prolly get flamed for this but oh well, bring it on. If someone is streetracing, wrecks and gets him/herself hurt, Even if it is a friend of mine, oh well. Theres no use me feeling bad about it or thinking street racing is a sin. The people who street race make it thier decision how far to push it, and make it thier decision to do it in the first place. I see no reason why I should b!tch and moan about someone getting hurt over something I had no control over. specially if it was a decision of the person(s) involved. If it is someone who wasn't involved in the streetrace, now then I'd feel bad. If I got hurt cuz I was racing someone in my 88 I wouldn't feel bad cuz I was hurt, I'd feel bad cuz I hurt my baby.. Love Muh 88  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Anyways, I'll stop running my mouth. bring on the heat, I'm ready!<P>------------------<BR>DjKid - 88 Coupe<BR>'Blue Knight"||||reg||1
Z||000011||Zorba||09-01-2000||02:08 AM||||We have this innate thing about "winning" even imaginary races. Street racing is simply a way for one guy to tell another that his car is faster, better, etc. The car is an extension of one's ego. Hence if the car wins, that person feels like they won something. It's personal satisfaction to see your skills and engine upgrades ($$$) at work. I personally favor the sleeper type cars. The ones that can go o-60 in 4.0 seconds but you'd never know by looking at them.<BR>But street racing can kill you, the other guy or innocent people, so one's decisions can often affect other innocent people.<BR>After all it gets pretty boring driving the speed limit all the time. But it's a false sense of security inside that car.||||reg||1
Z||000012||HugGRRRFiero||09-01-2000||09:45 AM||||  Man, I wish I had that picture of a Chevette that was split in half when passing thru an intersection at the same time a guy street racing his Corvette couldn't stop for a red light at over 100mph...  Nuff said!<P>  Take it to a drag strip, then you won't have to worry about hurting anyone but yourself and your car...||||reg||13
Z||000013||Zorba||09-01-2000||10:24 AM||||I agree.  But things get even worse when you are driving fast, especially if you have a small car that gets hit by a bigger one. A small car like the fiero being hit by a bigger car is nothing to laugh at:<P>Here's an excerpt of another traffic stupidity:<P> Police seek driver who left scene of fatal car accident <BR>      Salt Lake City police Thursday morning were looking for a driver who left the scene of a fatal accident.<BR>      A 70-year-old West Valley woman was killed when her car collided with another vehicle at the intersection of 1700 South and 300 West just after 5 a.m. Police don't know what caused the accident.<BR>(Let me guess, they were going slow!)<P>      Lt. Jim Jensen said witnesses saw the driver of the other vehicle walk over to the woman's car and then get back into his vehicle and leave.<BR>      Investigators say the man was wearing shorts and no shoes. <P>(typical around here, although not proven to cause wrecks)<P>He was last seen driving a white Blazer that has damage to the front and left side of the vehicle.<P><BR> <BR>||||reg||13
Z||000014||GT Bastard||09-02-2000||08:28 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Zorba:<BR><B>We have this innate thing about "winning" even imaginary races. Street racing is simply a way for one guy to tell another that his car is faster, better, etc. The car is an extension of one's ego. Hence if the car wins, that person feels like they won something. It's personal satisfaction to see your skills and engine upgrades ($$$) at work. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If you truely believe this, than you'll never understand.  When I pull up next to someone and the circumstances are right, I will meet a challenge even if I KNOW for a fact I will lose.  It's a thrill... For me it's not about ego's.  I regularly race other Fiero's in a local group that consists of about 5 people and their cars.  Nobody brags about who beats who.  It's just pure excitement, nothing else. Sure it's nice to win when somebody is cocky about their car, but that isn't what it's all about.  Granted, some street racers don't have the common sense they're born with, but many do it responsibly.  <P>||||reg||9
Z||000015||fropuff||09-02-2000||08:45 PM||||I agree.  Most people don't really rub it in when they beat you at a race.  There are many factors that determine the outcome.  Like, when my brother races (only to about 60) there aren't people that pull up to you, roll down the window, and rub your face in it.  There are always those people that you want to teach a lesson to and beat them, but you let it subside.  That way, you're both winners. (Don't make fun of me for sounding like Barney.)  I think that the ones that rub it in your face are the younger kids.  They just get their driver's licenses and want to show you their abilities.  When you get older, the feeling of accomplishment from beating someone in a race fades away.  But, what do I know; I'm only 15!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
